AB  - The goal of resilient software testing is to guarantee that software systems continue to work as intended in the face of failure, changes in their environment, or unexpected inputs. Software resilience under stress is evaluated using methods like adaptive test techniques, mutation testing, and chaos engineering to improve dependability in hostile or ever-changing environments. Automating and improving software quality assurance is the goal of this paper's agentic testing system, which makes use of artificial intelligence agents. To create context aware test cases, assess the effectiveness of patches, and discover regressions in real-time, the proposed approach blends reinforcement learning, fuzzy logic, knowledge graph reasoning, and large language models (LLMs). Agentic architecture allows for human-in-the-loop review for continual improvement and dynamically adapts to changing codebases, unlike conventional static testing techniques. To run a plethora of tests on the Defects4J dataset, which contains 835 actual Java errors. When contrasted with EvoSuite, the framework's results showed an 89% test coverage, an 84% mutation score, and a 55% reduction in test generation time. It also showed an 86% regression detection rate, a 91% accuracy rate for patch verification, and a very low false positive rate of only 8%. By a wide margin, fuzzy validation outperformed conventional methods, with an 82% developer acceptance rate and a contextual relevance score of 0.88. Notably, scores surpassing 75% were achieved in the areas of cross-project generalization, noisy tolerance, and idea drift adaptability. Positioned as a next generation solution for continuous integration pipelines in modern software engineering environments, the results underscore the framework's ability to deliver robust, explainable, and scalable testing automation.
ER  - 

